> That doesn't _seem_ to make sense.

You are absolutely right.  It does not and what more is not correct. :-)

> I ought only to need to be able to get a Linux kernel up and running.
> The Linux kernel is obviously able to address an AHA-2940, and,
> thereby, reference the existing filesystems.

This is the case.  But you have to load your kernel from some device
which is accessible, in term of a hardware support and of a sofware
layout, to firmware which you have on hand.  This DOES NOT have to
be a hard disk with your root file system but still some device you
can boot from.  Depending on circumstances this may be a floppy
or CD (updating a kernel on CD is more complicated operation) and
solutions like that were used by some.  Or maybe you could boot
from a network.  This is usually reasonably easy to achieve with
SRM if you have another machine (it does not have to be Alpha)
which can function as a boot server.  If your SRM variant can boot
from IDE then on occassions people were sticking old "throwaway"
IDE drives to serve as boot devices.  You can put also a secondary
swap there.  Reasonably new Alphas have built-in IDE controllers
decent enough that what was connected to them can be use as
"real disks" as well without bogging down your whole machine.

OTOH if you absolutely-positively have to boot from _that_ particular
hard disk on which your some file system live (be that / or something
else) then this disk has to be hooked up to a controller your firmware
knows about and you need a different kind of a partition table on that
disk for SRM than for ARC.

In some circumstances it is possible to rewrite a partition table
without touching partitions themselves (and their data) but such tricks
are in a "do not attempt at home" class (if you know how to do that you
do not ask question on c.o.l.a. :-) and usually you are much safer by
backing your data, reinstalling system, and restoring what needs to be
restored.

  Michal


