Compaq reprieves Alpha RH Linux

Too little, too late
By Mike Magee, 20/11/2001 09:33:45 BST


A BADLY NEEDED INJECTION of funds from Compaq has ensured that Red Hat now
has enough money to create version 7.2 for the Alpha microprocessor.

But the move is almost certainly too late to help satisfy anything but the
after market, after repeated Compaq, API and Samsung bungling, reported here
and at the Other Plaice.

Earlier we reported that Red Hat had told system integrators that with
uncertainty hanging over the future of the Alpha chip, it was considering
not developing 7.2 for the chip.

But Compaq's interest appears to have been re-kindled by its arch-competitor
Dell's ability to swing deals with Red Hat, and funds have now been found to
subsidise the port, sources tell us.

The move is unlikely to enflame the hearts and minds of system integrators
with renewed passion. A mere two years back believed that the combination of
API, Samsung and the Linux OS offered a reasonable opportunity for a high
performing and fast server platform, however.

Integrators now get their UP 1500 boards and CPUs from a Samsung - whose
interest in the chip is flagging. Now API has exited the market they get
UP2000+ and Slot B equipment from Microway.

Despite the platforms having huge performance advantages at the time over
the opposition, vendors held prices too high and failed to market products
properly. Many integrators switched to dual Athlons earlier this year when
motherboards became available.

The Compaq-Intel deal appeared to be the last straw for many of these
companies, particularly after Samsung refused to step in and be a white
knight for future development.

It had troubles enough of its own, and senior officials voided their rheums
