Erik Saarts <erik@ut.ee> writes:

> > bootp. The idea is to replace the Athlon file server by an Alpha
> > server which should support large files due to it's 64 bit
> > architecture. NFS3 also claims to support large files. But will
> > the (Athlon-) clients be able to handle these large files provided
> > over NFS without recompiling tools like cp, cat, ...? What about
> > Samba - any problems with large files there?  Thanks for your
> > help,
>
> Thats filesystem issue (limitation of ext2 filesystem).

Not really. The file size limit for ext2 with 4k blocks (as it is
commonly used nowadays) is 2 TB.  The question is mainly whether you
have a glibc supporting 64 bit file offsets, and if all the utils and
applications were compiled with appropriate flags. This depends on
your distribution. See e. g. http://www.suse.de/~aj/linux_lfs.html.

        Falk



