Brian T. Parr <btparr@starband.net> wrote:

> I'm running Redhat 7.0 on a PWS500a.  After upgrading the kernel from
> 2.2.17-4 to 2.4.9-6 (and more recently to 2.4.9-12) I get a "modprobe -
> can find module char-major-10-135" message during bootup.

This is RTC (do 'ls -l /dev/ | grep " 10,"' and see for yourself;
pay attention if this is a character or a block device).  Your kernel may
not have that module because either this support is not loaded as a
module or was not configured at all but something in your startup
files checks if it can peek there.

> Everything
> seems to work okay, but what does this message mean and how can I
> correct it?

How about a bold proposition to type 'man modules.conf' followed
by 'modprobe -c | less'?  You may even, surprise!, see a line
'alias char-major-10-135 rtc'.

You may alias that to "off" if you want, and this will kill that
"can't find" message, but if you would ever load a kernel where you
would want to have that module you would have to remember to turn
that on (can be done conditionally).

  Michal


